# ============================================================================
#  SE3090 Lecture 04 - LankaMart API request book
#
#  HOW TO USE
#    VS Code : install the "REST Client" extension, then click "Send Request"
#              above any request. Named captures below work automatically.
#    VS 2022 : this file opens natively; click the green arrow.
#    Rider   : opens natively.
#
#  THE FASTEST PATH THROUGH THE LECTURE
#    1.  run  #2 Login as Amal            -> copies a Customer token
#    2.  run  #14 Place an order          -> works
#    3.  run  #17 Read SOMEONE ELSE's order -> 403, the whole of slide 36
#    4.  run  #21 Try to add a product    -> 403, the whole of slide 38
#    5.  run  #3 Login as Staff, repeat #21 -> 201
#
#  Requests are numbered so the TEACHING_GUIDE.md can point at them.
# ============================================================================

@host = http://localhost:5090
@json = application/json

# Demo accounts - all four share this password in Development only.
@adminEmail    = admin@lankamart.lk
@staffEmail    = staff@lankamart.lk
@amalEmail     = amal@mail.lk
@nadiaEmail    = nadia@mail.lk
@password      = Password123!


# ############################################################################
#  PART 3 - THE API IS ALIVE, AND TALKING TO POSTGRESQL
# ############################################################################

### 1. Public catalogue - no token needed. Watch the SQL in the terminal.
GET {{host}}/api/v1/products?page=1&pageSize=5
Accept: {{json}}

### 1b. Filter and search - both become a WHERE clause, not a C# loop.
GET {{host}}/api/v1/products?search=mouse&page=1&pageSize=10
Accept: {{json}}

### 1c. Categories, so you know which CategoryId to send later.
GET {{host}}/api/v1/categories
Accept: {{json}}

### 1d. Which PostgreSQL are we on, and as WHICH USER? (slide 23)
#   If current_user is 'postgres' you are connected as the superuser.
GET {{host}}/api/v1/demo/db-info
Accept: {{json}}


# ############################################################################
#  PART 4 - AUTHENTICATION                                    (SLIDES 30-33)
# ############################################################################

### 2. Login as a CUSTOMER. Everything below reuses this token.
# @name loginAmal
POST {{host}}/api/v1/auth/login
Content-Type: {{json}}

{
  "email": "{{amalEmail}}",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}

@amalToken   = {{loginAmal.response.body.accessToken}}
@amalRefresh = {{loginAmal.response.body.refreshToken}}

### 3. Login as STAFF.
# @name loginStaff
POST {{host}}/api/v1/auth/login
Content-Type: {{json}}

{
  "email": "{{staffEmail}}",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}

@staffToken = {{loginStaff.response.body.accessToken}}

### 4. Login as ADMIN.
# @name loginAdmin
POST {{host}}/api/v1/auth/login
Content-Type: {{json}}

{
  "email": "{{adminEmail}}",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}

@adminToken = {{loginAdmin.response.body.accessToken}}

### 5. Login as a SECOND customer - needed for the 403 demo.
# @name loginNadia
POST {{host}}/api/v1/auth/login
Content-Type: {{json}}

{
  "email": "{{nadiaEmail}}",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}

@nadiaToken = {{loginNadia.response.body.accessToken}}

### 6. WRONG PASSWORD -> 401.
#   Read the message. Now run #7 and compare. They are IDENTICAL, on purpose:
#   different messages would let an attacker enumerate registered emails.
POST {{host}}/api/v1/auth/login
Content-Type: {{json}}

{
  "email": "{{amalEmail}}",
  "password": "definitely-not-the-password"
}

### 7. UNKNOWN EMAIL -> 401, the same message, in roughly the same time.
POST {{host}}/api/v1/auth/login
Content-Type: {{json}}

{
  "email": "nobody-here@mail.lk",
  "password": "definitely-not-the-password"
}

### 8. What did the token actually say? (slide 31, step 6)
#   Nothing here touched the database - this identity came from the JWT.
GET {{host}}/api/v1/auth/me
Authorization: Bearer {{amalToken}}
Accept: {{json}}

### 9. Decode the token WITHOUT any key at all. (slide 30)
#   Signed, not encrypted. Anyone can read it; only the key holder can forge it.
GET {{host}}/api/v1/demo/token-inspect?token={{amalToken}}
Accept: {{json}}

### 10. TAMPERED TOKEN -> 401. Change one character of the payload and the
#   signature no longer matches. Paste your own token and edit a letter.
GET {{host}}/api/v1/auth/me
Authorization: Bearer eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9.eyJzdWIiOiI5OTkiLCJyb2xlIjoiQWRtaW4ifQ.not-a-real-signature
Accept: {{json}}

### 11. NO TOKEN AT ALL -> 401 (not 403). Slide 28: we do not know who you are.
GET {{host}}/api/v1/auth/me
Accept: {{json}}

### 12. REFRESH - rotation. The old refresh token dies the moment this runs.
# @name refreshAmal
POST {{host}}/api/v1/auth/refresh
Content-Type: {{json}}

{
  "refreshToken": "{{amalRefresh}}"
}

### 13. REPLAY THE OLD REFRESH TOKEN -> 401 + the whole family is revoked.
#   Run #12 first, then run this. It sends the SAME token again, which is what
#   a thief would do. The API cannot tell a thief from a replay, so it assumes
#   theft: every token in the family is revoked and both parties must log in
#   again. Check the terminal for "Refresh token REUSE detected". (slide 32)
POST {{host}}/api/v1/auth/refresh
Content-Type: {{json}}

{
  "refreshToken": "{{amalRefresh}}"
}

### 13b. Register a brand-new customer. Note: adding "role": "Admin" here
#   changes nothing - RegisterRequest has no such field. (slide 40)
POST {{host}}/api/v1/auth/register
Content-Type: {{json}}

{
  "name": "Test Student",
  "email": "student.test@mail.lk",
  "password": "StudentPass123!",
  "role": "Admin",
  "isActive": true
}


# ############################################################################
#  PART 5 - AUTHORIZATION                                     (SLIDES 36-38)
# ############################################################################

### 14. Place an order as Amal. There is no customerId in this body -
#   it comes from the token's 'sub' claim, server-side. (slide 33, last row)
POST {{host}}/api/v1/orders
Authorization: Bearer {{amalToken}}
Content-Type: {{json}}

{
  "paymentMethod": "card",
  "items": [
    { "productId": 1, "quantity": 2 },
    { "productId": 4, "quantity": 1 }
  ]
}

### 15. OVER-POSTING PROOF. Same request, now claiming to be customer 1.
#   The field is ignored. Check the customerId in the response.
POST {{host}}/api/v1/orders
Authorization: Bearer {{nadiaToken}}
Content-Type: {{json}}

{
  "customerId": 1,
  "status": "DELIVERED",
  "deliveryFee": 0,
  "paymentMethod": "cash",
  "items": [
    { "productId": 2, "quantity": 1 }
  ]
}

### 16. Amal's own orders. This endpoint CANNOT return anyone else's data:
#   the filter is the token's user id, so there is no id to tamper with.
GET {{host}}/api/v1/orders/my
Authorization: Bearer {{amalToken}}
Accept: {{json}}

### 17. ****  THE OBJECT-LEVEL AUTHORIZATION DEMO  ****        (slide 36)
#   Order 1 belongs to Amal (seeded). Ask for it as NADIA.
#   Valid token. Same Customer role. Same endpoint. -> 403 Forbidden.
#   Comment out one line in OrderService.EnsureCanAccess and this becomes a
#   data breach. OWASP API Security Top 10, number one.
GET {{host}}/api/v1/orders/1
Authorization: Bearer {{nadiaToken}}
Accept: {{json}}

### 18. The same order, as its OWNER -> 200.
GET {{host}}/api/v1/orders/1
Authorization: Bearer {{amalToken}}
Accept: {{json}}

### 19. The same order, as STAFF -> 200. Role beats ownership here, by design.
GET {{host}}/api/v1/orders/1
Authorization: Bearer {{staffToken}}
Accept: {{json}}

### 20. Every order in the system. Customer token -> 403. Staff -> 200.
GET {{host}}/api/v1/orders?page=1&pageSize=10
Authorization: Bearer {{amalToken}}
Accept: {{json}}

### 20b. ...and now as Staff.
GET {{host}}/api/v1/orders?page=1&pageSize=10
Authorization: Bearer {{staffToken}}
Accept: {{json}}

### 21. ****  ROLE-BASED AUTHORIZATION  ****                   (slide 38)
#   Add a product as a CUSTOMER -> 403. Authenticated, identified, refused.
POST {{host}}/api/v1/products
Authorization: Bearer {{amalToken}}
Content-Type: {{json}}

{
  "name": "Contraband Widget",
  "categoryId": 1,
  "price": 999,
  "stockQty": 5
}

### 22. The identical request as STAFF -> 201 Created.
POST {{host}}/api/v1/products
Authorization: Bearer {{staffToken}}
Content-Type: {{json}}

{
  "name": "Laptop Sleeve 15 inch",
  "categoryId": 1,
  "price": 3200,
  "stockQty": 20
}

### 23. DELETE is Admin-only. As Staff -> 403.
DELETE {{host}}/api/v1/products/11
Authorization: Bearer {{staffToken}}

### 24. As Admin -> 204... unless the product has been ordered, in which case
#   PostgreSQL refuses (ON DELETE RESTRICT) and you get 409. (slide 14)
DELETE {{host}}/api/v1/products/11
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}

### 25. Deleting a product that IS on an order -> 409 Conflict.
#   The database defends the invoice from the application.
DELETE {{host}}/api/v1/products/1
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}

### 26. PERMISSION-based, not role-based.                     (slide 38, note)
#   Refund requires the "orders.refund" permission claim. Staff have it.
POST {{host}}/api/v1/orders/1/refund
Authorization: Bearer {{staffToken}}
Accept: {{json}}

### 27. The same refund as a Customer -> 403. No permission claim in the token.
POST {{host}}/api/v1/orders/1/refund
Authorization: Bearer {{amalToken}}
Accept: {{json}}

### 28. Cancel your own order -> 200, and the stock goes back.
POST {{host}}/api/v1/orders/2/cancel
Authorization: Bearer {{amalToken}}
Accept: {{json}}

### 29. Admin: list users and their roles.                        (slide 37)
GET {{host}}/api/v1/users?page=1&pageSize=10
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}
Accept: {{json}}

### 30. Admin: grant Staff to Amal - ONE row in the user_roles junction table.
POST {{host}}/api/v1/users/3/roles
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}
Content-Type: {{json}}

{
  "role": "Staff"
}

### 31. ****  THE STATELESS TRADE-OFF  ****                       (slide 37)
#   Amal is Staff now. Run #21 again with his EXISTING token: still 403,
#   because roles were baked into the token at login. Then run #2 to log in
#   again and repeat - now it works. Revocation lag, in thirty seconds.
POST {{host}}/api/v1/products
Authorization: Bearer {{amalToken}}
Content-Type: {{json}}

{
  "name": "Second Widget",
  "categoryId": 1,
  "price": 450,
  "stockQty": 3
}

### 32. Admin: revoke the role again. This ALSO kills Amal's refresh tokens,
#   so the removal cannot be outlived by a quiet renewal.
DELETE {{host}}/api/v1/users/3/roles/Staff
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}

### 33. Log out - revokes the refresh token server-side.
POST {{host}}/api/v1/auth/logout
Content-Type: {{json}}

{
  "refreshToken": "{{amalRefresh}}"
}


# ############################################################################
#  VALIDATION, ERRORS AND THE DELIBERATELY WRONG CODE       (SLIDES 26, 40, 41)
# ############################################################################

### 34. Validation failure -> 400 with a field-by-field list, before any of
#   our code runs. Empty name, negative price, unknown payment method.
POST {{host}}/api/v1/products
Authorization: Bearer {{staffToken}}
Content-Type: {{json}}

{
  "name": "",
  "categoryId": 1,
  "price": -50,
  "stockQty": -3
}

### 35. Business rule -> 409. Order more than the stock we hold.
POST {{host}}/api/v1/orders
Authorization: Bearer {{amalToken}}
Content-Type: {{json}}

{
  "paymentMethod": "card",
  "items": [ { "productId": 5, "quantity": 99 } ]
}

### 36. A real PostgreSQL 23505 unique violation -> 409, cleanly. (slide 26)
GET {{host}}/api/v1/demo/unique-violation
Accept: {{json}}

### 37. An unexpected exception -> 500 with a traceId and NOTHING else.
#   Then find that traceId in the terminal, with the full stack. (slide 41)
GET {{host}}/api/v1/demo/boom
Accept: {{json}}

### 38. What a careless API would have returned instead. (simulation only)
GET {{host}}/api/v1/demo/leaky-error
Accept: {{json}}

### 39. ****  SQL INJECTION  ****                                 (slide 40)
#   Baseline: one product.
GET {{host}}/api/v1/demo/injection?name=Mouse&mode=vulnerable
Accept: {{json}}

### 40. The same endpoint, with  ' OR '1'='1  as the search text.
#   Count the rows. The input stopped being data and became SYNTAX.
GET {{host}}/api/v1/demo/injection?name=' OR '1'='1&mode=vulnerable
Accept: {{json}}

### 41. Identical input, parameterized. Zero rows - it searched for a product
#   literally named "' OR '1'='1", which is exactly right.
GET {{host}}/api/v1/demo/injection?name=' OR '1'='1&mode=safe
Accept: {{json}}

### 42. N+1 - one query for the orders, then one per order. Count them.
GET {{host}}/api/v1/demo/n-plus-one
Accept: {{json}}

### 43. The same data in a single JOIN.
GET {{host}}/api/v1/demo/with-include
Accept: {{json}}

### 44. bcrypt: same password, two different hashes, both valid. (slide 31)
GET {{host}}/api/v1/demo/hash-password?password=Password123!
Accept: {{json}}
